  "image_name": "0dae65bff29b17aa2c8cebcb27597d15.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how CRISPR–Cas9 can be used to build cancer-relevant genome models through different DNA repair outcomes. It is organized around three broad strategies.\n\nOne branch uses Cas9 with a single guide RNA to create double-strand breaks repaired by non-homologous end joining, producing loss-of-function edits. This enables knockout of individual tumor suppressors, oncogenes, or drug-response genes, as well as multiplex knockouts to test combinations, epistatic relationships, and synthetic lethal interactions.\n\nA second branch adds a DNA donor so repair occurs by homology-directed repair, allowing precise sequence changes. The examples shown include introducing defined gain-of-function point mutations such as in Kras, engineering conditional alleles with recombinase sites, inserting tags or fluorescent reporters into endogenous genes, and altering non-coding regulatory DNA to model promoter or enhancer mutations that change gene expression.\n\nThe third branch uses Cas9 with two guide RNAs to generate larger genomic rearrangements. The schemes depict deletions, inversions, and chromosomal translocations, including well-known oncogenic fusions such as EML4–ALK and BCR–ABL1.\n\nOverall, the image presents CRISPR as a flexible toolkit for recreating many classes of cancer mutations, from small edits to complex structural variants.",
